00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:53.470
"Do hats slash caps cause baldness?"

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.610
In very specific circumstances.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.780
Meaning that if you're wearing
a really tight baseball cap

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.360
or some kind of skullie
where you're actually

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.110
putting traction on your hair,

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.720
that can cause a type of
baldness called traction alopecia

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.060
I think that's exactly what it's called.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.940
Where you're actually
pulling out your hairs

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.350
from that constant pressure.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.010
But otherwise, if you wear
a hat that's well fitted

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.810
and not overly tight,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.680
it's not gonna make you lose your hair.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.972
In fact, most men will incorrectly assume

00:03:19.972 --> 00:03:22.680
that this can happen because
they like wearing hats,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.390
they experience male-pattern baldness,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.500
which is a hormonal issue,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.860
has nothing to do with the hats.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.480
And then they'll go
ahead and blame the hats.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.990
That's an area of
correlation versus causation.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.090
Like men wearing hats at a certain age

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.140
experiencing baldness at that same age,

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.350
they think oh look, these
two things happened together,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.130
they must cause one another, they don't.

